{
181: {skill_tested: "Identify the narrator's initial assumption.", question: "At the beginning of the passage, the narrator assumes that the pale patch should be:", option1: "removed along with the chair arm.", option2: "dated through laboratory testing.", option3: "stained until it is no longer visible.", option4: "displayed separately from the chair.", correctAnswer: "stained until it is no longer visible.", explanation: "Step 1: The narrator sees the patch interrupting the dark grain.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator initially believes the obvious task is to stain it until it disappears.<br><br>Answer: stained until it is no longer visible.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
182: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of an interpretive term.", question: "As used in the final paragraph, the word 'legible' most nearly means:", option1: "protected from all physical contact.", option2: "identical to its original condition.", option3: "marked with a precise date.", option4: "capable of being interpreted or understood.", correctAnswer: "capable of being interpreted or understood.", explanation: "Step 1: The final paragraph describes a material record containing damage, repair, and use.<br><br>Step 2: Keeping that record legible means preserving the ability to interpret its evidence.<br><br>Answer: capable of being interpreted or understood.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
183: {skill_tested: "Identify the passage's central argument.", question: "Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", option1: "Conservation requires judging which changes carry useful evidence and how honestly that evidence can be presented.", option2: "Conservation should make every object appear newly manufactured.", option3: "Later repairs are always more important than an object's original design.", option4: "Museums should display only furniture with documented repair dates.", correctAnswer: "Conservation requires judging which changes carry useful evidence and how honestly that evidence can be presented.", explanation: "Step 1: Repairs may reveal use and decisions but may also damage or mislead.<br><br>Step 2: Context, documentation, and evidentiary limits guide treatment and display.<br><br>Answer: Conservation requires judging which changes carry useful evidence and how honestly that evidence can be presented.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
184: {skill_tested: "Analyze the function of a series of examples.", question: "The drawer runner, shortened table leg, and covered upholstery in the third paragraph primarily serve to:", option1: "prove that all old furniture was poorly designed.", option2: "show several ways furniture records practical choices made after manufacture.", option3: "list repairs that conservators are required to remove.", option4: "explain why walnut is unsuitable for chairs.", correctAnswer: "show several ways furniture records practical choices made after manufacture.", explanation: "Step 1: Each example is an alteration prompted by wear, environment, fashion, or protection.<br><br>Step 2: Together they support the claim that objects pass through a chain of decisions.<br><br>Step 3: The list broadens the argument beyond the single walnut chair.<br><br>Answer: show several ways furniture records practical choices made after manufacture.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
185: {skill_tested: "Explain why documentation precedes irreversible treatment.", question: "Why does the author emphasize documenting a harmful repair before removing it?", option1: "Documentation guarantees that the repair will become structurally harmless.", option2: "Written observations can replace the original object in every exhibition.", option3: "Documentation can preserve evidence that the physical treatment will destroy.", option4: "Measurements prove who made every undocumented repair.", correctAnswer: "Documentation can preserve evidence that the physical treatment will destroy.", explanation: "Step 1: Some later work must be removed to protect an object.<br><br>Step 2: Removal can eliminate information contained in that work.<br><br>Step 3: Recording it preserves access to evidence even when the material cannot remain.<br><br>Answer: Documentation can preserve evidence that the physical treatment will destroy.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
186: {skill_tested: "Infer the effect of exhibition purpose on interpretation.", question: "The discussion of the repaired school desk implies that the same physical feature may be:", option1: "visible only when a classroom has limited funds.", option2: "evidence of standardized construction in every context.", option3: "removed whenever its maker is unknown.", option4: "historically useful in one exhibition and distracting in another.", correctAnswer: "historically useful in one exhibition and distracting in another.", explanation: "Step 1: In a history classroom, the repair can prompt discussion of maintenance and budgets.<br><br>Step 2: In a manufacturing exhibition, it may distract from standardized construction.<br><br>Step 3: Interpretive relevance changes with the question an exhibition asks.<br><br>Answer: historically useful in one exhibition and distracting in another.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
187: {skill_tested: "Analyze how a treatment embodies the author's argument.", question: "The final treatment of the walnut chair is best described as an attempt to:", option1: "balance visual coherence with the continued visibility of repair evidence.", option2: "make the patch dominate the chair from every viewing distance.", option3: "restore the chair to a provably exact original color.", option4: "assign the patch a detailed story despite missing evidence.", correctAnswer: "balance visual coherence with the continued visibility of repair evidence.", explanation: "Step 1: Conservators reduce the patch's strongest contrast.<br><br>Step 2: They leave knife marks visible at close range and label the repair cautiously.<br><br>Step 3: The treatment makes the chair readable without erasing or overstating the patch.<br><br>Answer: balance visual coherence with the continued visibility of repair evidence.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
188: {skill_tested: "Evaluate the passage's distinction between evidence and interpretation.", question: "Which statement best explains the claim that evidence does not announce its own importance?", option1: "Physical marks have no meaning until they are completely removed.", option2: "The relevance assigned to a repair depends partly on the questions and context through which it is presented.", option3: "Only notable craftspeople create changes worth documenting.", option4: "Every mark on an object supports the same historical conclusion.", correctAnswer: "The relevance assigned to a repair depends partly on the questions and context through which it is presented.", explanation: "Step 1: The same type of repair can matter differently depending on maker, object, and exhibition purpose.<br><br>Step 2: Curators select and explain evidence within a particular frame.<br><br>Step 3: Material presence alone does not determine which historical question it answers.<br><br>Answer: The relevance assigned to a repair depends partly on the questions and context through which it is presented.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
189: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the narrator's revised understanding of conservation.", question: "By describing conservation as a less tidy responsibility, the narrator most nearly means that conservation:", option1: "is unsuccessful unless all signs of age disappear.", option2: "should preserve every alteration regardless of structural harm.", option3: "requires choices among competing forms of value rather than a simple reversal of change.", option4: "can avoid interpretation by documenting each object.", correctAnswer: "requires choices among competing forms of value rather than a simple reversal of change.", explanation: "Step 1: Change can contain damage, evidence of use, and later craftsmanship at once.<br><br>Step 2: Treatments may protect structure, clarify design, or preserve later history, and those aims can conflict.<br><br>Step 3: Conservation therefore involves justified choices and evidentiary restraint, not merely making age vanish.<br><br>Answer: requires choices among competing forms of value rather than a simple reversal of change.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
